1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:06,000
It keeps us warm. It keeps us cool. It protects us.

2
00:00:06,001 --> 00:00:11,000
As simple as double-paned glass or as complex as the shell of a space station.

3
00:00:11,001 --> 00:00:15,000
It's all around us, but it's almost always out of sight.

4
00:00:15,001 --> 00:00:19,000
It's an essential part of almost every building we inhabit.

5
00:00:19,001 --> 00:00:23,000
Now, Insulation on Modern Marvels.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:41,300
It's the secret weapon in the war on energy consumption.

7
00:00:43,420 --> 00:00:46,100
With a worldwide energy crisis upon us,

8
00:00:46,840 --> 00:00:51,380
scientists and contractors are scrambling to design and build more fuel-efficient buildings.

9
00:00:54,540 --> 00:00:59,520
Energy-saving insulation is key to winning any battle waged at the fuel pump.

10
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:06,000
Revolutionary products like ICFs, or insulated concrete forms,

11
00:01:06,001 --> 00:01:11,000
are changing the way we're constructing 21st century buildings.

12
00:01:11,001 --> 00:01:16,000
Think of them as Lego blocks for big kids.

13
00:01:16,001 --> 00:01:19,000
They snap together like Lego blocks.

14
00:01:19,001 --> 00:01:23,000
They provide a superior installation envelope for buildings,

15
00:01:23,001 --> 00:01:25,000
homes, schools, churches.

16
00:01:25,001 --> 00:01:29,000
They also provide an air barrier and high soundproofing.

17
00:01:29,001 --> 00:01:33,500
The primary insulation material of the forms is rigid foam.

18
00:01:33,501 --> 00:01:41,300
The white part that you see is typically expanded polystyrene made from a base product called

19
00:01:41,301 --> 00:01:42,300
styropore.

20
00:01:42,301 --> 00:01:43,940
It looks like sugar.

21
00:01:43,941 --> 00:01:46,020
It's a sugar plastic.

22
00:01:46,021 --> 00:01:52,160
The forms come with a diamond on the top and a circle on the bottom.

23
00:01:52,161 --> 00:01:56,240
So the diamonds typically go up and the circles go down.

24
00:01:56,241 --> 00:02:04,460
You snap these together, they snuck right in.

25
00:02:04,461 --> 00:02:07,360
And you can see this horizontal seam right here.

26
00:02:07,600 --> 00:02:11,780
That's where the two have sucked together, and it's become an interlocked system.

27
00:02:12,360 --> 00:02:19,820
Next, the blocks are hooked together and fitted with rebar in preparation for the concrete pouring part of the process.

28
00:02:23,480 --> 00:02:25,840
The concrete is poured using a boom pump.

29
00:02:26,240 --> 00:02:29,860
It comes down into the hose.

30
00:02:29,861 --> 00:02:33,400
We pour about a block and a half to two blocks at a time.

31
00:02:33,401 --> 00:02:42,480
That's typically about 32 to 36 inches.

32
00:02:42,481 --> 00:02:48,540
Once completed, the building has a double layer of insulation built right into its structure.

33
00:02:48,541 --> 00:02:52,540
The insulated concrete forms make it much more efficient than traditional insulation

34
00:02:52,541 --> 00:02:53,540
processes.

35
00:02:53,541 --> 00:02:59,680
To make the analogy, if you have a really hot cup of coffee that's poured into a Styrofoam

36
00:02:59,681 --> 00:03:02,640
cup, you're able to hold onto that cup.

37
00:03:02,641 --> 00:03:07,980
And the reason why is because you've got a very thin layer of foam between you and that

38
00:03:07,981 --> 00:03:09,700
scalding cup of coffee.

39
00:03:09,701 --> 00:03:18,100
If you take that into context of that thickness of Styrofoam cup versus this thickness, multiplied

40
00:03:18,101 --> 00:03:25,180
twice as a wrap for your building, you get a sense of the incredible insulation values

41
00:03:25,181 --> 00:03:30,240
that you're giving yourself to protect from the external environment.

42
00:03:30,241 --> 00:03:34,480
This efficiency immediately registers on the building's energy bills.

43
00:03:34,481 --> 00:03:39,140
The number one consumer of energy on this planet is the heat and cool structures to

44
00:03:39,141 --> 00:03:41,500
keep humans comfortable in.

45
00:03:41,501 --> 00:03:46,980
And with an insulated concrete form envelope in that building, you're conservatively cutting

46
00:03:46,981 --> 00:03:52,100
that energy usage down by at least 30 to 40 percent versus wood frame structures.

47
00:03:53,140 --> 00:03:56,660
Today, ICFs are among the fastest growing insulation products.

48
00:03:59,700 --> 00:04:06,340
ICFs have the same goal as all forms of insulation, to effectively control heat flow by trapping air.

49
00:04:08,500 --> 00:04:13,860
In a typical three-bedroom house, 25 percent of heat is lost through an uninsulated roof,

50
00:04:13,861 --> 00:04:17,280
15% through uninsulated floors,

51
00:04:18,200 --> 00:04:20,680
25% through windows and doors,

52
00:04:21,480 --> 00:04:25,420
and 35% through uninsulated walls.

53
00:04:27,080 --> 00:04:29,500
Effective insulation can put an end to these losses.

54
00:04:30,620 --> 00:04:34,140
Insulation is basically just a material that slows down heat flow.

55
00:04:34,460 --> 00:04:40,080
So that means it can be used to prevent heat from flowing into an area that you want to have cool

56
00:04:40,081 --> 00:04:42,820
or flowing out of an area that you want to keep warm.

57
00:04:43,860 --> 00:04:46,820
Heat energy transfer takes place in three ways.

58
00:04:47,820 --> 00:04:50,560
Conduction, convection, and radiation.

59
00:04:52,620 --> 00:04:59,180
Conduction carries heat through molecules that are in direct contact with one another in an object, like a metal poker.

60
00:05:00,800 --> 00:05:07,540
Convection transfers heat by physically moving molecules from one place to another, such as when warm air rises.

61
00:05:07,541 --> 00:05:15,800
And radiation carries heat in the form of waves through space, even if the space is a vacuum.

62
00:05:18,220 --> 00:05:21,520
Effective insulation controls all of these heat transfer methods.

63
00:05:22,800 --> 00:05:26,320
Today, insulation is a $22 billion a year industry.

64
00:05:28,520 --> 00:05:33,140
Modern insulation products are a quantum leap from those employed by early human beings.

65
00:05:33,141 --> 00:05:39,880
insulation going back in history probably originally was clothing that people would wear

66
00:05:39,881 --> 00:05:46,960
whether it was a skin or a coat made out of animal fur that type of thing and insulation in their

67
00:05:46,961 --> 00:05:53,160
homes was probably just the uh you know the roof of the cave over their head or or something

68
00:05:53,161 --> 00:05:58,280
something real crude as time went on people started to figure out how to put more material

69
00:05:58,281 --> 00:06:00,720
between them and the outside environment.

70
00:06:01,160 --> 00:06:03,420
It may have been leaves or grass

71
00:06:03,421 --> 00:06:06,260
in some of the early structures.

72
00:06:08,020 --> 00:06:11,360
Earth itself is a millennia-old form of insulation.

73
00:06:13,240 --> 00:06:16,680
The Egyptians used underground chambers to stay cool,

74
00:06:17,400 --> 00:06:19,280
and that was a wonderful way to keep cool

75
00:06:19,281 --> 00:06:23,620
and using the Earth's mass as its own regulated insulator.

76
00:06:24,840 --> 00:06:27,120
Surprisingly, asbestos was an important

77
00:06:27,121 --> 00:06:29,120
early insulation material.

78
00:06:29,121 --> 00:06:35,160
Asbestos is a naturally occurring fiber within the minerals of the earth.

79
00:06:35,161 --> 00:06:39,560
It's up to debate really who first used asbestos, but it's guaranteed that the Greeks were the

80
00:06:39,561 --> 00:06:45,540
first to actually name it, and that Greek translation was not extinguishable.

81
00:06:45,541 --> 00:06:49,700
All cultures searched for building materials with tiny enclosed spaces in them to reduce

82
00:06:49,701 --> 00:06:51,980
heat flow.

83
00:06:51,981 --> 00:06:55,060
In Spain and Portugal, the favored material was cork.

84
00:06:55,061 --> 00:07:00,440
It was probably the first renewable insulation product because once a cork oak tree reaches

85
00:07:00,441 --> 00:07:04,940
the age of 25 years, the bark can be skinned every 9 to 10 years.

86
00:07:04,941 --> 00:07:10,880
It'll replenish itself, but that cork, having that cellular structure is an excellent insulator.

87
00:07:10,881 --> 00:07:14,860
In Arctic regions, snow was used as insulation.

88
00:07:14,861 --> 00:07:21,500
The Eskimos found that dry snow has millions of entrained air cells in a snow capsule,

89
00:07:21,501 --> 00:07:28,720
And so they actually built snow blocks for their homes, which also became an excellent insulator because of the air that was trapped within the snow.

90
00:07:30,660 --> 00:07:34,240
Inhabitants of tropical climates used materials generated by volcanoes.

91
00:07:34,880 --> 00:07:40,180
In Hawaii, I think you'll find probably one of the first uses of mineral wool insulation.

92
00:07:40,360 --> 00:07:44,860
And what that actually started from was a mineral fiber, and it was naturally created by the volcanoes.

93
00:07:45,100 --> 00:07:50,740
When steam was released from the volcano, it actually turned the lava into fluffy fibers.

94
00:07:50,741 --> 00:07:56,260
And they found they could mix that into mud and other products to create their own insulation mass.

95
00:07:56,720 --> 00:08:04,380
In the 16th and 17th centuries, when building construction evolved from solid walls to framed walls with cavities in them,

96
00:08:05,400 --> 00:08:09,500
people began filling the cavities with everything from corn cobs to sawdust.

97
00:08:11,420 --> 00:08:14,440
With insulation, necessity was the mother of invention.

98
00:08:14,441 --> 00:08:16,460
during the 19th century.

99
00:08:17,600 --> 00:08:21,160
American homesteaders on the plains used sod to insulate their houses,

100
00:08:22,020 --> 00:08:24,920
a practice that continued into the early 20th century.

101
00:08:26,480 --> 00:08:30,480
Folks have been very resourceful in what they want to use for insulation.

102
00:08:31,180 --> 00:08:33,160
Many times it's just whatever they had on hand.

103
00:08:34,700 --> 00:08:38,300
Thatched huts in northern Europe were built with a roof of woven straw

104
00:08:38,301 --> 00:08:41,760
up to two feet thick and walls of straw and clay.

105
00:08:44,440 --> 00:08:52,140
It wasn't until the Industrial Revolution in the 19th century that deliberate commercial applications of thermal insulation became mainstream.

106
00:08:54,480 --> 00:08:57,240
Mineral wool became one of the most popular products.

107
00:08:57,820 --> 00:09:01,440
Mineral wool is a very broad definition.

108
00:09:01,740 --> 00:09:07,360
It basically covers any type of insulation made from glass, rock, or slag.

109
00:09:07,361 --> 00:09:17,891
And I think that you found all these, I guess, new technologies becoming much more popular after the Industrial Revolution because of the processes that allowed easier

110
00:09:17,892 --> 00:09:18,900
access to these.

111
00:09:20,160 --> 00:09:23,580
Molten slag was a byproduct of the iron ore blast furnace.

112
00:09:25,440 --> 00:09:30,620
Iron workers found that injecting it with steam accelerated the cooling and solidification process.

113
00:09:30,621 --> 00:09:39,380
This acceleration expanded the structure of the slag, producing fluffy fibers that could be turned into mineral wool.

114
00:09:43,340 --> 00:09:49,420
In the early 20th century, builders also began to use specially designed insulation boards.

115
00:09:50,180 --> 00:09:57,140
These were created by processing wood, cane, or vegetable chips into a pulp that was pressurized into boards.

116
00:09:57,141 --> 00:10:04,480
In the 1930s, the insulation industry developed a wonder called fiberglass.

117
00:10:05,740 --> 00:10:10,640
The technique of heating and drawing glass into fine fibers has been known since ancient times.

118
00:10:11,800 --> 00:10:17,500
However, the first glass fibers of the type we know today as fiberglass were made by accident.

119
00:10:18,780 --> 00:10:23,840
In 1932, Dale Kleist, a researcher for Corning Glass,

120
00:10:23,841 --> 00:10:27,080
was trying to weld together two glass blocks

121
00:10:27,081 --> 00:10:31,720
when a jet of compressed air inadvertently hit a stream of molten glass

122
00:10:31,721 --> 00:10:34,460
and created a shower of glass fibers.

123
00:10:35,900 --> 00:10:41,420
The resulting spray of fine fibers turned out to be very light and very strong.

124
00:10:42,880 --> 00:10:49,280
In 1935, Corning Glass joined forces with Owens, Illinois to develop the idea further.

125
00:10:50,520 --> 00:10:53,180
Research showed that glass was a poor conductor of heat.

126
00:10:53,840 --> 00:10:56,380
and tightly woven glass fibers trapped air.

127
00:10:57,460 --> 00:10:59,800
With its ability to do both of these things,

128
00:11:00,260 --> 00:11:02,340
fiberglass proved to be an excellent insulator.

129
00:11:04,860 --> 00:11:09,320
Today, Owens Corning is one of the largest fiberglass insulation producers in the world.

130
00:11:12,500 --> 00:11:15,480
The whole process of creating fiberglass insulation

131
00:11:15,481 --> 00:11:20,220
begins with the traditional batch of materials used to create most forms of glass.

132
00:11:23,840 --> 00:11:29,160
We're behind an electric melt cold top furnace.

133
00:11:29,161 --> 00:11:31,780
This furnace is melting the batch materials.

134
00:11:31,781 --> 00:11:37,960
Those materials have about 50% sand, about 40% recycled plate and bottle cullet, and

135
00:11:37,961 --> 00:11:42,060
about 10% sodash, limestone and borax.

136
00:11:42,061 --> 00:11:46,800
These materials are melted underneath from the batch cover by electric heating.

137
00:11:46,801 --> 00:11:49,000
It's a temperature of around 2400 degrees.

138
00:11:49,001 --> 00:11:54,940
and then the glass flows out into the channel towards the fiberization process.

139
00:11:54,941 --> 00:11:59,580
The temperature of the glass is tightly controlled within tolerances as well as the flow rate

140
00:11:59,581 --> 00:12:07,280
of the glass as it is entering the fiberization process, which is essentially a spinning process,

141
00:12:07,281 --> 00:12:14,120
very similar to a cotton candy machine.

142
00:12:14,121 --> 00:12:16,960
Owens Corning makes pink insulation.

143
00:12:16,961 --> 00:12:21,740
The color pink is actually a registered trademark of the company.

144
00:12:21,741 --> 00:12:25,140
It uses the color to visually distinguish its product from other brands.

145
00:12:25,141 --> 00:12:31,060
After the liquid glass is spun into fiber, we spray on a resin.

146
00:12:31,061 --> 00:12:36,400
This fiber and resin and dye is collected onto a conveyor, which feeds into our curing

147
00:12:36,401 --> 00:12:39,000
oven.

148
00:12:39,001 --> 00:12:43,240
Fiberglass insulation is so soft because the fibers themselves are 20 times thinner than

149
00:12:43,241 --> 00:12:48,740
human hair, making them flexible and resilient.

150
00:12:48,741 --> 00:12:53,960
After the material is cured in the oven and the thickness is set, the fiberglass pack

151
00:12:53,961 --> 00:12:57,360
is conveyed down the line to the fabrication area.

152
00:12:57,361 --> 00:13:03,140
Behind me is the chopping area where we are cutting the bats to length to meet the various

153
00:13:03,141 --> 00:13:06,480
product specifications required by our customers.

154
00:13:06,481 --> 00:13:12,920
Here we are in the bat packaging area.

155
00:13:12,921 --> 00:13:17,260
We are mechanically compressing the bat to remove the air and reduce the overall volume

156
00:13:17,261 --> 00:13:18,260
of the package.

157
00:13:18,261 --> 00:13:23,700
This is really an important step in being able to store more material in our warehouse

158
00:13:23,701 --> 00:13:31,320
as well as to enable easier shipping and handling to our customers.

159
00:13:31,321 --> 00:13:32,680
This is the end of the line.

160
00:13:32,681 --> 00:13:36,860
The time it took from when the liquid glass was fiberized until it got down here in a

161
00:13:36,861 --> 00:13:41,220
finished bat bag was less than five minutes.

162
00:13:41,221 --> 00:13:46,840
The assembly line at this Owens Corning plant often runs 24 hours a day and produces enough

163
00:13:46,841 --> 00:13:56,060
fiberglass to insulate almost 175,000 homes per year.

164
00:13:56,061 --> 00:14:01,640
Today fiberglass insulation comes in many different forms.

165
00:14:01,641 --> 00:14:06,640
One of the most popular is bats, which are pre-cut to a specific shape and thickness

166
00:14:06,641 --> 00:14:10,640
in order to fit in the standard framing of a building.

167
00:14:10,641 --> 00:14:16,740
Another very effective form of fiberglass insulation is Johns Manville's spray-in spider

168
00:14:16,741 --> 00:14:18,680
insulation.

169
00:14:18,681 --> 00:14:25,200
When applying spider insulation, you'll see the installer with a blowing hose, which will

170
00:14:25,201 --> 00:14:30,860
lead back to a blowing machine where the loose-fill fiberglass has been placed.

171
00:14:30,861 --> 00:14:34,960
It'll be ground up and conveyed through the hose to the nozzle.

172
00:14:35,600 --> 00:14:39,760
There'll also be a line delivering tacky adhesive, and that'll be mixed at the nozzle.

173
00:14:40,180 --> 00:14:48,280
He'll direct that material mixture into the wall and work the nozzle back and forth to build up the insulation to fill up the whole cavity.

174
00:14:50,540 --> 00:15:00,367
His assistant will come along behind the sprayer with a scrubbing tool, a roller that he'll run down the face of the studs to scrub off any excess to leave a nice flat

175
00:15:00,368 --> 00:15:01,068
layer.

176
00:15:01,069 --> 00:15:03,200
right at the front surface of the studs.

177
00:15:03,660 --> 00:15:08,640
The adhesive used with the insulation will quickly solidify and dry,

178
00:15:09,020 --> 00:15:13,400
and the wall is ready for finishing with drywall almost immediately.

179
00:15:14,460 --> 00:15:18,260
Then any excess that's remaining on the floor will be vacuumed up,

180
00:15:18,261 --> 00:15:20,660
and it will be recycled back into the hopper,

181
00:15:21,020 --> 00:15:23,840
where it will be sprayed into the wall again further down the line.

182
00:15:23,980 --> 00:15:27,280
So when you're done with the job, there's no waste, no leftover material.

183
00:15:27,281 --> 00:15:34,280
Another kind of fiberglass insulation especially useful for insulating attics is loose fill.

184
00:15:34,281 --> 00:15:42,280
Loose fill is blown using a special machine through a hose into an attic area or sometimes

185
00:15:42,281 --> 00:15:47,270
into a wall. It basically stacks up and fills that area with insulation.

186
00:15:47,271 --> 00:15:49,280
The advantage of a loose fill

187
00:15:49,281 --> 00:15:55,280
insulation in an attic for instance is that it will fill in around all the gaps and cracks around

188
00:15:55,281 --> 00:15:58,280
around framing and other obstructions that might be up in there.

189
00:15:58,281 --> 00:16:04,280
The name of the game in the insulation business is filling any holes that allow thermal energy

190
00:16:04,281 --> 00:16:05,840
to escape.

191
00:16:05,841 --> 00:16:12,280
One extremely effective substance starts small but grows to get the big job done.

192
00:16:12,281 --> 00:16:15,740
Foam.

193
00:16:15,741 --> 00:16:20,620
The Trans-Alaska pipeline is wrapped with four inches of fiberglass insulation, which keeps

194
00:16:20,621 --> 00:16:27,620
the oil warm while it flows 800 miles to a refinery.

195
00:16:28,400 --> 00:16:34,567
It's pumped up, it's gooey, and it's one of the best insulators available.

196
00:16:34,568 --> 00:16:35,400
It's foam.

197
00:16:37,080 --> 00:16:42,330
Foam products come in many forms. One of the most effective is spray.

198
00:16:42,331 --> 00:16:44,080
The process of spraying

199
00:16:44,081 --> 00:16:47,780
The process of spraying foam insulation is almost an art form.

200
00:16:49,160 --> 00:16:55,100
It takes a steady eye, a sure hand, and a definite sense of rhythm.

201
00:16:57,320 --> 00:16:59,440
Foam is an exceptional insulator for roofs.

202
00:17:00,360 --> 00:17:05,200
Roof insulation foam offers significant advantages over alternative materials.

203
00:17:05,201 --> 00:17:08,440
It forms a seamless membrane which stops leaks.

204
00:17:08,441 --> 00:17:14,760
The material flows into cracks, crevices, expands, and seals up all the leaks.

205
00:17:14,761 --> 00:17:19,800
It also offers much more insulation value versus alternative materials.

206
00:17:19,801 --> 00:17:25,240
And the fact that it's soaked into all the cracks and crevices and cured means that it's

207
00:17:25,241 --> 00:17:29,240
gluing everything together, so it's going to be able to withstand significantly more

208
00:17:29,241 --> 00:17:32,040
harsh weather conditions than a typical roof would.

209
00:17:32,041 --> 00:17:38,360
The key to making a smooth urethane application when we're talking about roofing is to overlap

210
00:17:38,361 --> 00:17:41,400
each previous pass by 80 to 90 percent.

211
00:17:42,120 --> 00:17:43,160
When you're spray painting,

212
00:17:43,640 --> 00:17:45,620
normally you'll think about a 50 percent overlap.

213
00:17:46,220 --> 00:17:48,600
When spraying urethanes, you think of 80 or 90 percent.

214
00:17:49,040 --> 00:17:52,220
Because urethane expands 30 times its liquid form,

215
00:17:52,600 --> 00:17:55,320
that becomes critical if you want to get a smooth application.

216
00:17:56,960 --> 00:17:58,900
The idea of surrounding a building

217
00:17:58,901 --> 00:18:01,480
with a complete envelope of insulation like foam

218
00:18:01,481 --> 00:18:04,020
has been growing since the 1920s,

219
00:18:04,140 --> 00:18:07,180
when the way we constructed buildings began to change.

220
00:18:08,360 --> 00:18:11,700
Prior to the 1920s, even going back to the Victorian age,

221
00:18:11,760 --> 00:18:15,720
you found that homes were constructed of heavier, denser materials.

222
00:18:15,860 --> 00:18:19,600
A lot of that was just the prevalence of old-growth forests that were used for framing.

223
00:18:20,320 --> 00:18:22,500
By the time the 1920s rolled around,

224
00:18:22,560 --> 00:18:26,900
you found that materials were becoming lighter so they could be constructed more quickly.

225
00:18:27,500 --> 00:18:32,420
But there also became a greater emphasis on the idea of the thermal envelope of a residence.

226
00:18:32,880 --> 00:18:35,840
You start to insulate the floors and the walls and the roofs.

227
00:18:35,841 --> 00:18:41,900
The use of building insulation was first made mandatory in the United States during

228
00:18:41,901 --> 00:18:47,600
World War II to conserve fuel for the war effort.

229
00:18:47,601 --> 00:18:52,160
You all of a sudden had to start being more concerned about conserving your energy resources,

230
00:18:52,161 --> 00:18:54,960
whether it be oil or gasoline.

231
00:18:54,961 --> 00:18:59,000
And because of that, they looked at better applications of insulation.

232
00:18:59,001 --> 00:19:02,340
That same example was driven home in the 70s.

233
00:19:02,341 --> 00:19:06,665
reacted to oil prices and gas prices and energy prices going up.

234
00:19:06,666 --> 00:19:08,760
The 1970s saw the advent of the

235
00:19:08,761 --> 00:19:11,362
idea of the super insulated home.

236
00:19:11,363 --> 00:19:15,700
Foam is a substance that is formed by trapping many gas

237
00:19:15,701 --> 00:19:21,875
bubbles in a liquid or solid. One of the most popular foams is polyurethane.

238
00:19:21,876 --> 00:19:23,500
Originally developed

239
00:19:23,501 --> 00:19:29,500
during the 1940s for military and aviation applications, it began to be used as insulation

240
00:19:29,501 --> 00:19:30,920
during the 1950s.

241
00:19:31,460 --> 00:19:35,460
In the 1970s, innovators began spraying the material.

242
00:19:37,080 --> 00:19:39,520
Polyurethane is made of several key ingredients.

243
00:19:40,140 --> 00:19:43,860
These are the materials you see stored in the large tanks behind me.

244
00:19:44,500 --> 00:19:45,500
This is a polyol.

245
00:19:45,860 --> 00:19:49,720
It's based on sucrose, or sugar, and petroleum products.

246
00:19:50,820 --> 00:19:52,520
This is a surfactant.

247
00:19:52,700 --> 00:19:56,060
Surfactant controls the size and the shape of the bubbles.

248
00:19:56,061 --> 00:20:00,680
This is a catalyst which controls reaction rate.

249
00:20:01,520 --> 00:20:03,260
The quicker the reaction, the smaller the bubbles.

250
00:20:05,120 --> 00:20:09,460
The size of the bubbles is important because it dictates the density of the foam.

251
00:20:11,660 --> 00:20:16,300
Roofing foam, which might be walked on, needs to be stronger and denser than wall foam,

252
00:20:16,640 --> 00:20:18,620
which is rarely touched after it's applied.

253
00:20:20,980 --> 00:20:25,520
All of this is factored into the formula when a batch of foam material is mixed at the factory.

254
00:20:26,060 --> 00:20:30,560
But where do the bubbles come from?

255
00:20:30,561 --> 00:20:31,960
The blowing agent.

256
00:20:31,961 --> 00:20:37,720
The blowing agent is a liquid that has a low boiling point.

257
00:20:37,721 --> 00:20:43,840
When it's spray applied, it converts from a liquid to a gas forming the bubbles.

258
00:20:43,841 --> 00:20:48,140
These four ingredients are mixed together into one liquid, but they're only part of

259
00:20:48,141 --> 00:20:51,020
the recipe for making foam.

260
00:20:51,021 --> 00:21:01,540
The other part is a substance called isocyanate, a highly reactive molecule.

261
00:21:01,541 --> 00:21:05,020
It's separated from the other ingredients until the spraying begins.

262
00:21:05,021 --> 00:21:11,440
They're dispensed separately all the way through the hoses to the gun.

263
00:21:11,441 --> 00:21:16,900
When the applicator pulls the gun, the materials are mixed together, the reaction occurs.

264
00:21:16,901 --> 00:21:23,260
It hits the substrate in a liquid form, but the reaction drives the expansion very, very

265
00:21:23,261 --> 00:21:29,400
quickly. Within seconds, you've got cellular plastic.

266
00:21:29,401 --> 00:21:33,460
Isonene is a foam insulation that's used to create an envelope of protection around a

267
00:21:33,461 --> 00:21:37,220
building.

268
00:21:37,221 --> 00:21:42,300
We come into a house, we will seal around the doors and the windows, we will plastic everything

269
00:21:42,301 --> 00:21:45,470
off and then we'll spray the entire house.

270
00:21:45,471 --> 00:21:48,640
We'll spray the walls, the floors, and the

271
00:21:48,641 --> 00:21:53,960
roof deck and completely encase the house in foam.

272
00:21:53,961 --> 00:21:58,620
A properly insulated house can save a homeowner a great deal of money.

273
00:21:58,621 --> 00:22:03,140
The consumer today is better educated as far as the value of better insulation in their

274
00:22:03,141 --> 00:22:03,841
home.

275
00:22:03,842 --> 00:22:07,280
What they have learned is they can pay a little bit more on the front end to better

276
00:22:07,281 --> 00:22:11,960
insulate their home, but they will recoup savings in the long term versus their energy

277
00:22:11,961 --> 00:22:14,560
costs because their house is better insulated.

278
00:22:16,180 --> 00:22:20,960
Today, insulation-savvy home and business owners are finding that there are savings

279
00:22:20,961 --> 00:22:26,100
to be gained from insulating not only the walls of a building, but the windows, too.

280
00:22:28,900 --> 00:22:33,980
Each year in the United States, nearly $13 billion worth of energy in the form of heated

281
00:22:33,981 --> 00:22:43,020
or cooled air escapes through holes and cracks in residential buildings.

282
00:22:43,021 --> 00:22:48,640
Designers of today's superstructures strive to insulate every important section.

283
00:22:48,641 --> 00:22:52,800
One critical area includes the windows.

284
00:22:52,801 --> 00:22:57,360
There's little to be gained from insulating the interior walls and ceilings of a structure

285
00:22:57,361 --> 00:23:03,920
when all the thermal energy passes easily through the windows.

286
00:23:03,921 --> 00:23:07,920
At the Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory in Berkeley, California,

287
00:23:07,921 --> 00:23:13,920
scientists are working to develop windows with superior insulating properties.

288
00:23:13,921 --> 00:23:18,920
The lab is managed by the University of California.

289
00:23:18,921 --> 00:23:24,920
One major advance in window insulation came with the introduction of double-paned windows.

290
00:23:24,921 --> 00:23:29,920
For centuries, single-pane glass was the standard in buildings throughout the country.

291
00:23:29,921 --> 00:23:37,460
However, in the 1960s, window manufacturers began to experiment with and develop insulating glass,

292
00:23:37,620 --> 00:23:45,240
which are two pieces of glass separated at the edges by a spacer with the airspace sealed from the outside

293
00:23:45,241 --> 00:23:49,460
so that no moisture can condense between the two pieces of glass.

294
00:23:50,420 --> 00:23:55,320
Insulated glass became much more popular in the United States after the early 70s energy crisis.

295
00:23:55,321 --> 00:24:00,320
This is a standard double glazed window, which means that it has two pieces of glass.

296
00:24:00,321 --> 00:24:07,320
The two pieces of glass create a dead air space, and it's that air that's really the insulator in this window system.

297
00:24:07,321 --> 00:24:15,320
Air's thermal conductivity is very low compared to other solids, which means that the rate of heat flowing through it is low.

298
00:24:15,321 --> 00:24:17,320
That's why it's a great insulator.

299
00:24:17,321 --> 00:24:20,320
To further improve the glass's insulation qualities,

300
00:24:20,321 --> 00:24:27,320
manufacturers added a low-emittance coating to reduce heat flow through it.

301
00:24:27,321 --> 00:24:29,337
Air is a good insulator.

302
00:24:29,338 --> 00:24:37,320
But in the 1980s, some manufacturers began filling their panes with heavy gases, such as argon.

303
00:24:37,321 --> 00:24:46,320
Air is a good insulator because the molecules that comprise air, nitrogen, oxygen, are relatively large, simple molecules.

304
00:24:46,321 --> 00:24:51,800
molecules, molecules transfer energy from one molecule to another through colliding

305
00:24:51,801 --> 00:24:55,640
with each other and transferring energy with those collisions.

306
00:24:55,641 --> 00:25:00,820
Bigger molecules like argon move slower and so the rate at which they transfer energy

307
00:25:00,821 --> 00:25:06,320
from one molecule to another is lower and therefore their conductivity is lower, the

308
00:25:06,321 --> 00:25:12,260
rate at which the whole space transfers heat is lower.

309
00:25:12,261 --> 00:25:19,060
In the Berkeley labs, each new glass insulation design is run through a battery of tests.

310
00:25:19,061 --> 00:25:21,580
This is our window thermal test facility.

311
00:25:21,581 --> 00:25:25,820
If you look back there, you can see two windows framed in red.

312
00:25:25,821 --> 00:25:31,440
Those windows separate this warm side chamber from a cold exterior chamber.

313
00:25:31,441 --> 00:25:36,160
And with our special imaging equipment, we can look at the surface temperatures of these

314
00:25:36,161 --> 00:25:37,160
windows.

315
00:25:37,161 --> 00:25:42,060
Now by understanding what the surface temperature patterns are, we can tell how good an insulator

316
00:25:42,061 --> 00:25:44,660
the windows are.

317
00:25:44,661 --> 00:25:48,840
Scientists also use infrared tests.

318
00:25:48,841 --> 00:25:53,240
With this infrared camera, we see the infrared radiation

319
00:25:53,241 --> 00:25:56,300
that is one of the forms of heat transfer.

320
00:25:56,301 --> 00:26:00,780
It can tell us the performance of a thermal insulation

321
00:26:00,781 --> 00:26:06,560
by side-by-side testing, such as with these three windows.

322
00:26:06,561 --> 00:26:10,920
Here we have a single pane of glass, a pour insulator.

323
00:26:10,921 --> 00:26:12,880
You can see its temperature is very cold

324
00:26:12,881 --> 00:26:17,220
because the color indicates the surface temperature.

325
00:26:17,221 --> 00:26:20,920
Here we have a double pane of glass separated by airspace

326
00:26:20,921 --> 00:26:22,840
that's a better insulator.

327
00:26:22,841 --> 00:26:28,580
And best performing triple pane insulating glass is up here.

328
00:26:28,581 --> 00:26:31,640
The different colors you see in the infrared image

329
00:26:31,641 --> 00:26:34,280
represent different surface temperatures,

330
00:26:34,281 --> 00:26:37,840
which is an indication of the performance of the glazings.

331
00:26:37,841 --> 00:26:44,840
A major breakthrough in insulated glass was the development of gas-filled panels in the

332
00:26:44,841 --> 00:26:45,840
1990s.

333
00:26:45,841 --> 00:26:51,840
We realized that the same principles that were being used to create highly insulating

334
00:26:51,841 --> 00:26:58,840
windows could be applied to creating a high-performance thermal insulation that was lightweight and

335
00:26:58,841 --> 00:27:01,840
that utilized a small amount of materials.

336
00:27:01,841 --> 00:27:06,840
This is a gas-filled panel developed here at Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory.

337
00:27:06,841 --> 00:27:14,040
And here is a similar panel with the edge cut away so we can see the inner workings of the gas-filled panel.

338
00:27:14,560 --> 00:27:16,820
This material here is called the baffle.

339
00:27:17,280 --> 00:27:20,080
It creates numerous gas or air-filled spaces.

340
00:27:20,760 --> 00:27:24,220
These small spaces are effective at reducing the transfer of heat.

341
00:27:25,020 --> 00:27:31,020
The panels have many uses, including insulating automobiles, food containers, and buildings.

342
00:27:31,021 --> 00:27:37,020
This is an example of a gas-filled panel that's been developed for use in buildings.

343
00:27:37,021 --> 00:27:40,020
This particular product here is designed for use in attics,

344
00:27:40,021 --> 00:27:43,020
and it's meant to augment existing ceiling insulation

345
00:27:43,021 --> 00:27:46,020
that might not be quite enough insulation for today's homeowner.

346
00:27:46,021 --> 00:27:49,020
This product comes from the factory in an uninflated state,

347
00:27:49,021 --> 00:27:52,020
so that it's very thin and can be transported easily.

348
00:27:52,021 --> 00:27:56,020
And to inflate it, you simply take an air compressor

349
00:27:56,021 --> 00:28:00,020
or some form of compressed air and inflate it.

350
00:28:01,020 --> 00:28:05,880
Most insulation is hidden from sight.

351
00:28:05,881 --> 00:28:09,920
It works silently and rarely draws attention to itself.

352
00:28:09,921 --> 00:28:18,000
Yet it must handle heat, cold, smoke, fire, and even acoustic variations.

353
00:28:18,001 --> 00:28:24,220
The only way manufacturers know their products are up to the task is to test them elaborately

354
00:28:24,221 --> 00:28:28,400
and dramatically.

355
00:28:28,401 --> 00:28:35,000
A gas-filled panel that is one inch thick can provide the same insulation performance as foam that is two inches thick.

356
00:28:38,320 --> 00:28:41,900
The purpose of insulation is to create a comfort zone in a building.

357
00:28:42,460 --> 00:28:48,560
Usually out of sight, concealed in the walls of a structure, insulation rarely crosses the minds of a building's inhabitants.

358
00:28:49,140 --> 00:28:52,680
Like plumbing and wiring, it's taken for granted until something goes wrong.

359
00:28:52,681 --> 00:29:00,280
Insulation manufacturers like Johns Manville strive to eliminate problems by testing every

360
00:29:00,281 --> 00:29:06,360
aspect of the material before it goes on the market.

361
00:29:06,361 --> 00:29:10,800
For instance, insulation is tested extensively to see how it reacts to fire.

362
00:29:10,801 --> 00:29:16,800
The ASTM E84 test is designed to measure the surface burning characteristics of building

363
00:29:16,801 --> 00:29:17,800
materials.

364
00:29:17,801 --> 00:29:22,140
What we do is we start a flame across the face of a building material, whether it's

365
00:29:22,141 --> 00:29:27,360
plywood, insulation, insulation facings, and we measure how long it takes for the flame

366
00:29:27,361 --> 00:29:29,500
to propagate across the surface of this material.

367
00:29:30,280 --> 00:29:34,380
We're also doing simultaneously is measuring the amount of smoke that comes off of this

368
00:29:34,381 --> 00:29:35,340
material as it burns.

369
00:29:35,840 --> 00:29:39,740
One of the things that we're concerned with is that as things burn, they give off smoke,

370
00:29:39,741 --> 00:29:45,100
and smoke is just as hazardous, if not more so, than the fire itself.

371
00:29:45,380 --> 00:29:50,140
We do this test method to make sure that the products that we manufacture are going to

372
00:29:50,141 --> 00:29:52,020
be safe for their intended use.

373
00:29:52,140 --> 00:30:01,140
Testing helps manufacturers, like Urethane Contractor Supply and Consulting, develop the right insulation for any job.

374
00:30:01,141 --> 00:30:14,140
UCSC invested in an extreme conditions test lab to test adhesion and other physical properties of our spray foams on very cold substrates, including concrete.

375
00:30:14,141 --> 00:30:19,080
And we've made significant improvements in our adhesion and other physical properties of our products.

376
00:30:19,520 --> 00:30:24,900
For example, in the Midwest, in the Northeast, in the spring, residential construction starts.

377
00:30:25,480 --> 00:30:26,300
They pour the basement.

378
00:30:27,100 --> 00:30:31,120
The basement walls are very cold, typically 30 or 40 degrees.

379
00:30:31,740 --> 00:30:35,520
Traditional spray polyurethane foam materials don't adhere well in that application.

380
00:30:36,100 --> 00:30:40,080
Our PolarPro is engineered to adhere to very cold substrates.

381
00:30:40,081 --> 00:30:45,140
Insulation is also tested to determine its R value.

382
00:30:45,880 --> 00:30:49,700
This is a government-required rating that indicates a product's insulating qualities.

383
00:30:51,260 --> 00:30:56,380
R value is a standard term that means resistance to heat flow.

384
00:30:56,920 --> 00:31:01,260
R value is the principal way of explaining the performance of insulation.

385
00:31:01,800 --> 00:31:07,560
Every home insulation product is rated through standard testing with a certain R value,

386
00:31:07,561 --> 00:31:11,520
so a consumer can compare one product to another in standard terms.

387
00:31:11,660 --> 00:31:16,480
So the higher the R-value, the more resistance to heat flow that the insulation is providing.

388
00:31:17,360 --> 00:31:21,840
Researchers also perform elaborate tests on an insulation's acoustic properties.

389
00:31:22,580 --> 00:31:27,260
When we talk about insulation, we don't have to talk only about thermal insulation.

390
00:31:27,420 --> 00:31:28,840
There's also acoustic insulation.

391
00:31:29,400 --> 00:31:35,180
This is very important in hotels, hospitals, when there's an element of privacy that's required.

392
00:31:35,680 --> 00:31:36,900
Acoustics are very important.

393
00:31:36,901 --> 00:31:46,440
If you look at the way we're building now with property values going up, the increase in townhomes, condominiums, you're very concerned about noise between units.

394
00:31:48,120 --> 00:31:55,700
Acoustic engineers at companies like Johns Manville often strive to include both thermal and acoustic properties in the installations they produce.

395
00:31:56,900 --> 00:32:03,809
Acoustical insulation, thermal insulation, they're very similar in the fact that they're made the same way, they're made on the same production line, and a lot of ways

396
00:32:03,810 --> 00:32:04,760
they do the same thing.

397
00:32:04,761 --> 00:32:07,940
what's good for sound a lot of times is good for thermal and vice versa.

398
00:32:09,020 --> 00:32:14,340
A specially designed reverberation room allows engineers to bounce sound off their products from every angle.

399
00:32:15,340 --> 00:32:19,920
When we walk through the door of our reverberation room, what we see is a lot of diffusers on the wall.

400
00:32:20,040 --> 00:32:23,400
There's a large rotating vein that's on the upper portion of this room.

401
00:32:23,900 --> 00:32:28,960
What we're doing is we're trying to create an environment where the sound pressure level throughout the room is going to be even,

402
00:32:29,080 --> 00:32:30,860
no matter where you're listening.

403
00:32:30,861 --> 00:32:36,860
Sound is a series of waves created by the vibration of an object, like a loudspeaker.

404
00:32:36,861 --> 00:32:41,860
Some insulating materials absorb sound waves by converting them to heat,

405
00:32:41,861 --> 00:32:46,860
which is produced by the friction of the vibrations passing through tiny openings in the material.

406
00:32:49,860 --> 00:32:52,860
One important test performed in the reverberation room

407
00:32:52,861 --> 00:32:56,860
measures the amount of sound reduction an insulation product can deliver.

408
00:32:56,861 --> 00:32:58,920
can deliver.

409
00:32:58,921 --> 00:33:01,240
How we test our material is that we place it

410
00:33:01,241 --> 00:33:03,100
inside the cavity of a wall.

411
00:33:03,101 --> 00:33:04,960
We put this wall in between two rooms.

412
00:33:04,961 --> 00:33:08,380
We energize one side of the wall with 100 decibels of sound.

413
00:33:08,381 --> 00:33:10,460
And then we measure how much of that sound goes through

414
00:33:10,461 --> 00:33:13,060
to the other side with another microphone.

415
00:33:13,061 --> 00:33:16,300
The technicians call the 100 decibels of sound pink noise.

416
00:33:21,540 --> 00:33:24,240
It's equivalent to hearing a jackhammer from just six feet

417
00:33:24,241 --> 00:33:24,941
away.

418
00:33:26,860 --> 00:33:35,580
Pink noise, if you can imagine a spectrum, you have frequencies ranging from 100 Hz all the way to 4000 Hz.

419
00:33:36,060 --> 00:33:39,480
And at each one of those discrete frequencies, we have 100 decibels of sound.

420
00:33:40,000 --> 00:33:44,620
That way, when we're looking at the difference going from one side to the other, we know we're always starting at 100,

421
00:33:45,140 --> 00:33:48,760
and we know that that sound is going to be reduced going from one side of the wall to the other.

422
00:33:48,761 --> 00:33:57,160
Another acoustic insulation testing room is anechoic, meaning it permits an unusually low degree of reverberation.

423
00:33:58,500 --> 00:34:03,700
What you see as you walk into our anechoic room is that the walls have fiberglass wedges on the walls.

424
00:34:04,160 --> 00:34:10,860
What these are designed to do is absorb all the sound in this room that radiates from whatever noise source that we're trying to measure.

425
00:34:10,861 --> 00:34:19,804
The advantage of this room is that we're using two microphones so we can measure not only the magnitude or the amount of sound coming off of whatever we're measuring,

426
00:34:19,805 --> 00:34:25,300
but we're also measuring the direction so we can make a map of sound that's coming off of this device.

427
00:34:25,740 --> 00:34:32,387
In this case, we have an appliance, and what we see is that we know that we don't get a lot of noise coming through the door, but we get a lot of it that comes out

428
00:34:32,388 --> 00:34:33,088
from underneath.

429
00:34:33,400 --> 00:34:40,740
Unfortunately, we need to have airflow underneath this device to make sure that it's cooled properly, but again, that's also a path for sound to come through.

430
00:34:40,860 --> 00:34:45,700
This gives us a way of finding how much sound is coming out from what part of this assembly.

431
00:34:52,280 --> 00:34:59,320
Once the sound is mapped in these rooms, the insulation can be altered accordingly to improve acoustic performance.

432
00:35:02,380 --> 00:35:06,920
We require different types of insulation to keep our homes comfortable here on Earth.

433
00:35:06,921 --> 00:35:11,920
But what if home is in outer space?

434
00:35:11,921 --> 00:35:16,920
Anechoic chambers can absorb 99.9% of sound.

435
00:35:16,921 --> 00:35:18,920
Because most ambient noise is eliminated,

436
00:35:18,921 --> 00:35:21,920
people have reported hearing their own hearts beating

437
00:35:21,921 --> 00:35:25,920
and blood flowing while inside a chamber.

438
00:35:29,920 --> 00:35:34,920
Insulation is helping us go places we could never go before,

439
00:35:34,921 --> 00:35:37,260
including extreme environments.

440
00:35:38,620 --> 00:35:41,860
When designing insulation for the International Space Station,

441
00:35:42,940 --> 00:35:46,280
scientists were confronted with a set of problems found nowhere on Earth.

442
00:35:47,080 --> 00:35:51,260
We don't have the benefit of Earth's atmosphere when we're in orbit.

443
00:35:51,860 --> 00:35:56,720
And not having that benefit, we're exposed to much more extreme environments.

444
00:35:57,640 --> 00:36:02,480
In the same orbit, we can see temperatures in excess of 250 degrees Fahrenheit

445
00:36:02,481 --> 00:36:08,380
while we're in the sun, while a few minutes later be exposed to conditions that produce environments

446
00:36:08,381 --> 00:36:11,626
less than minus 250 degrees Fahrenheit.

447
00:36:11,627 --> 00:36:17,120
And we go through this 16 times a day, every day, 365 days a year.

448
00:36:18,160 --> 00:36:22,240
Scientists had to rethink the entire insulation process when designing the station.

449
00:36:23,320 --> 00:36:28,400
The rules that govern air and heat movement on Earth simply don't apply in outer space.

450
00:36:28,401 --> 00:36:32,400
The Earth's atmosphere allows heat to be transferred by convection.

451
00:36:32,401 --> 00:36:36,400
But in space, there is no atmosphere.

452
00:36:36,401 --> 00:36:40,400
The vacuum of space allows only for radiant heat transfer,

453
00:36:40,401 --> 00:36:44,400
usually in the form of energy waves emanating from the sun.

454
00:36:44,401 --> 00:36:48,400
Earth's atmosphere prevents it from being overheated by these waves.

455
00:36:48,401 --> 00:36:52,400
But the space station, without such protection, is subject to the extreme

456
00:36:52,401 --> 00:36:56,400
temperatures brought on by solar radiation.

457
00:36:56,401 --> 00:37:00,420
The scientists had to come up with an insulation that would safeguard it.

458
00:37:01,060 --> 00:37:05,140
Their solution was a multi-layer insulation, or MLI.

459
00:37:06,160 --> 00:37:08,240
MLI serves two purposes.

460
00:37:08,480 --> 00:37:12,560
The first is to reject or reflect a lot of the sun's energy.

461
00:37:13,260 --> 00:37:17,300
And the second is to entrap heat that's generated internally.

462
00:37:17,820 --> 00:37:21,440
And by doing this, we can moderate the extremes that we see on orbit.

463
00:37:21,441 --> 00:37:26,440
One of the key components of the insulation is the plastic, mylar.

464
00:37:26,441 --> 00:37:31,440
On that plastic, we apply thin layers of aluminum,

465
00:37:31,441 --> 00:37:35,440
which is your typical aluminum can type of material.

466
00:37:35,441 --> 00:37:39,440
And that aluminum is very reflective.

467
00:37:39,441 --> 00:37:44,440
The mylar is protected by an outer layer of fiberglass fabric called beta cloth.

468
00:37:44,441 --> 00:37:49,440
This layer strengthens the insulation blanket.

469
00:37:49,441 --> 00:37:59,580
The inner side of the beta cloth is aluminized to prevent harmful radiation like ultraviolet light from drying out and cracking the mylar layers beneath.

470
00:38:00,580 --> 00:38:04,080
The next layer is a fine mesh fabric of Dacron.

471
00:38:05,020 --> 00:38:08,780
This prevents the conduction of heat between the aluminized layers of mylar.

472
00:38:10,020 --> 00:38:14,180
There are at least 10 more layers of aluminized mylar and Dacron per blanket.

473
00:38:14,181 --> 00:38:21,300
All the layers are sewn together to create a pillow-like blanket just one quarter of an inch thick.

474
00:38:23,120 --> 00:38:28,000
I'm going to show you a little bit about the multi-layer insulation that's installed on the space station right now.

475
00:38:28,340 --> 00:38:32,800
Here we have examples of large blankets which cover the end cone of the space station.

476
00:38:33,160 --> 00:38:37,840
Now they're held on with these little tiny buttons or grommets with some lacing thread.

477
00:38:38,280 --> 00:38:43,040
And now this acts to actually not conduct heat down to the skin of the space station itself.

478
00:38:43,041 --> 00:38:48,040
When designing the insulation, scientists also had to make sure it didn't weigh too much.

479
00:38:48,041 --> 00:38:53,040
We used very thin layers to make up multi-layer insulation.

480
00:38:53,041 --> 00:38:55,040
The reason mainly is because of weight.

481
00:38:55,041 --> 00:39:00,040
The pressure shell of the module itself is about a quarter inch thick on some of these modules.

482
00:39:00,041 --> 00:39:02,040
And that aluminum can be very heavy.

483
00:39:02,041 --> 00:39:05,040
Well, the last thing you want to do is add more weight to the space station.

484
00:39:05,041 --> 00:39:09,040
The insulation also had to be designed to be tough.

485
00:39:09,041 --> 00:39:11,400
because unlike most insulation on Earth,

486
00:39:12,480 --> 00:39:14,600
space station insulation takes a real beating.

487
00:39:16,140 --> 00:39:17,880
The space environment is very hostile.

488
00:39:18,260 --> 00:39:21,560
You have micrometeorites that pound the space station at all times.

489
00:39:21,960 --> 00:39:24,640
Here's an example of the meteoroid debris shield,

490
00:39:24,820 --> 00:39:26,960
which actually takes the hit from meteoroids

491
00:39:26,961 --> 00:39:30,400
to prevent the multilayer insulation from being damaged.

492
00:39:30,980 --> 00:39:34,120
Working in tandem with thermal controls inside the station,

493
00:39:34,640 --> 00:39:38,520
this advanced insulation helps to maintain a stable onboard environment

494
00:39:38,521 --> 00:39:43,340
with a temperature of 65 to 80 degrees Fahrenheit

495
00:39:43,341 --> 00:39:48,680
and humidity in the range of 25 to 70 percent.

496
00:39:50,700 --> 00:39:54,400
The extreme environment of outer space is approximated on Earth,

497
00:39:54,401 --> 00:39:55,860
at the bottom of the sea.

498
00:39:57,460 --> 00:39:59,720
Again, insulation is a key component,

499
00:40:00,180 --> 00:40:02,980
especially in the design of protective gear worn by divers.

500
00:40:04,120 --> 00:40:07,620
One of the first diving suits was introduced in 1837

501
00:40:07,621 --> 00:40:10,500
by Augustus Sieb, a German engineer.

502
00:40:13,160 --> 00:40:15,740
Early diving suit makers were much more interested

503
00:40:15,741 --> 00:40:17,040
in making their suits waterproof

504
00:40:17,041 --> 00:40:19,000
and easily supplied with air

505
00:40:19,001 --> 00:40:21,020
and insulated for the diver's comfort.

506
00:40:22,380 --> 00:40:25,120
However, as diving equipment became more sophisticated

507
00:40:25,121 --> 00:40:27,980
and divers stayed underwater for longer durations,

508
00:40:28,740 --> 00:40:30,260
the need for insulation grew.

509
00:40:31,500 --> 00:40:34,180
Without it, the cold water could seriously affect

510
00:40:34,181 --> 00:40:35,200
a diver's performance.

511
00:40:36,100 --> 00:40:37,440
He's going to fatigue a lot quicker

512
00:40:37,441 --> 00:40:39,980
because now he's shaking and shivering underwater.

513
00:40:40,480 --> 00:40:42,700
He's going to lose a little bit of his mental focus.

514
00:40:43,520 --> 00:40:47,280
Today, one of the most common diving suits is the membrane dry suit.

515
00:40:51,520 --> 00:40:55,220
Heat insulation is provided by the clothes a diver wears under the suit.

516
00:40:56,360 --> 00:41:01,120
These garments are often similar to those worn by skiers and mountain climbers in cold climates.

517
00:41:07,440 --> 00:41:12,720
As the name indicates, the suit keeps a diver dry after it's sealed at the neck and wrists.

518
00:41:14,020 --> 00:41:15,800
No water touches his body.

519
00:41:16,740 --> 00:41:19,120
This is the inner garment that's formed by the diver.

520
00:41:19,540 --> 00:41:21,640
It is referred to us as a woolly bear.

521
00:41:22,140 --> 00:41:27,080
It's a fleece-lined down suit that goes close to the diver's body

522
00:41:27,081 --> 00:41:32,080
and provides a layer of warmth between the diver and the outer garment, which is the dry suit.

523
00:41:33,300 --> 00:41:36,160
The wetsuit is another form of dive suit.

524
00:41:37,440 --> 00:41:43,680
It was made possible by neoprene, a stretchable and buoyant synthetic rubber invented in the

525
00:41:43,681 --> 00:41:44,520
1930s.

526
00:41:46,340 --> 00:41:49,900
The first wetsuits were marketed in the early 1950s.

527
00:41:50,960 --> 00:41:56,500
The neoprene wetsuit works by trapping a layer of water between the diver's body and the

528
00:41:56,501 --> 00:41:58,360
insulation material, which is the neoprene.

529
00:41:58,361 --> 00:42:03,500
This particular wetsuit is 5 millimeters thick, so there's a 5 millimeter layer insulating

530
00:42:03,501 --> 00:42:07,945
the diver from the ocean, then the layer of water, and then the diver's body.

531
00:42:07,946 --> 00:42:08,646
The diver's

532
00:42:08,647 --> 00:42:13,320
body warms the layer of water inside the suit, and that's what keeps him warm.

533
00:42:15,500 --> 00:42:21,820
For extreme dives to depths of 2,000 feet beneath the surface and temperatures near

534
00:42:21,821 --> 00:42:25,693
freezing, divers rely on the hard suit.

535
00:42:25,694 --> 00:42:30,360
The suit itself has no insulation, so the diver

536
00:42:30,361 --> 00:42:31,960
must wear special gear.

537
00:42:31,961 --> 00:42:36,680
The hard suit is much like a miniature submarine.

538
00:42:36,681 --> 00:42:40,180
The diver will get in and wear this thing like a suit.

539
00:42:40,181 --> 00:42:45,820
It has arms and legs with mechanical joints and he's protected from the environment by

540
00:42:45,821 --> 00:42:50,800
an aluminum suit, but he's not protected from the environment for cold.

541
00:42:50,801 --> 00:42:54,400
He has to wear additional insulation inside the hard suit.

542
00:42:54,401 --> 00:42:58,840
The hard suit diver will be insulated by up to four layers of protection, depending on

543
00:42:58,841 --> 00:43:01,980
the temperature of the water and the amount of time he's going to spend in the water.

544
00:43:01,981 --> 00:43:07,240
He starts with a polyester and spandex undergarment that wicks moisture away from his body so

545
00:43:07,241 --> 00:43:11,720
that as he works, if he develops sweat, that moisture is wicked away from his body.

546
00:43:11,721 --> 00:43:15,660
Follow that with a typical dive skin, which is sort of fleece-lined and has a little bit

547
00:43:15,661 --> 00:43:17,900
of a neoprene-type covering.

548
00:43:17,901 --> 00:43:23,160
And then he's got a fleece-lined, Gore-Tex-type arctic jacket, which provides the bulk of

549
00:43:23,161 --> 00:43:27,460
the insulation from the temperature, keeping his body heat in.

550
00:43:27,461 --> 00:43:32,460
And then finally with a completely watertight Gore-Tex outer garment that prevents moisture

551
00:43:32,461 --> 00:43:40,860
from getting in but allows his body to wick out, to breathe out.

552
00:43:40,861 --> 00:43:47,720
Whether beneath the sea or on a space station, insulation has always helped us create comfort

553
00:43:47,721 --> 00:43:48,720
zones.

554
00:43:48,721 --> 00:43:55,080
At home or in the office, it's essential for making our world habitable.

555
00:43:55,081 --> 00:44:02,320
How we use it and how we develop it will be of major importance in how we conserve our

556
00:44:02,321 --> 00:44:05,080
supply of energy in the coming years.

